

MODLOAD.CONF(5)      UNIX Programmer's Manual     MODLOAD.CONF(5)



NAME
     modload.conf - kernel modules loaded at boot

DESCRIPTION
     The file /etc/modload.conf lists the FGXM modules the kernel
     loads once the filesystem is up.  One module name per line,
     each resolved as /lib/modules/<name>.  Blank lines are
     ignored, as is any line whose first non-blank character is
     `#'.  The name is the first run of non-blank characters;
     anything after it on the line is ignored.

     Modules load top to bottom, so one that depends on another
     must follow it.  A module that fails to load is reported to
     the kernel log and LOADING CONTINUES with the next line -- a
     bad entry costs you that module, not the boot.  A missing
     file is not an error either.

     THE FILESYSTEM DOES NOT BELONG HERE: not prodos, and since
     the 2.11BSD root landed, not vfs, ufs or ufsdir either.  A
     module named here is read THROUGH a filesystem -- including
     the open of this file -- so a filesystem module named here
     could never be read at all.  The boot loader stages those
     instead; see fgxunix(5).

     What is left is every module that can wait until there is a
     root, which is where 4.3BSD runs modload(8) from as well: out
     of /etc/rc, once the filesystem is mounted.

     hello is a demonstration module: the smallest working example
     of the module interface.  It ships in /lib/modules and is
     commented out here.

FILES
     /etc/modload.conf
     /lib/modules

SEE ALSO
     fgxunix(5), boot.conf(5), init(8)



